🔐 Local Authority — Writeup
📋 What's the Challenge?
You're given a login page for a "secure customer portal." You need to log in as the admin.  But you don't know the username or password... or do you? 🤔

💡 Hint from the challenge: "How is the password checked on this website?" 

🎯 What You're Working With
Item	Value
🌐 Type	A login page (PHP)
🧩 Difficulty	Easy (Beginner)
🛠 Key Concept	Client-side code is NOT secret

🤔 The Big Idea (Super Simple)
Here's the key thing to understand:

🧠 Everything sent to your browser is visible. 

When a website loads, it sends you:

📄 HTML (the page structure)
🎨 CSS (the styling)
⚡ JavaScript (the logic/code) 
ALL of it is readable by you. The developer made a mistake here — they put the admin password in a JavaScript file that gets sent to every visitor. It's like putting your house key under the doormat and telling everyone "the key is under the doormat!" 🚪🔑

👀 Step 1: Try to Log In
Open the challenge URL. You'll see a login form.

Try anything:

Field	Value
Username	admin
Password	12345

Click Login → ❌ "Log In Failed" 

🤔 "How does the website check my password? On the server? Or...?" 

🔑 Step 2: View the Page Source
Right-click → "View Page Source" (or press Ctrl + U) 

You'll see HTML code with a bunch of JavaScript inside. Look for a line like this:

<script src="secure.js"></script>

🚨 Suspicious name! A file called secure.js?  Why is a "secure" file being sent to ME?

🔍 Step 3: Open the secure.js File
You have two ways to see it:

Method A: Click the link in the source
In the page source, right-click the secure.js link → "Open link in new tab" 

Method B: Use the Network tab (easier to find!)
Press F12 → Click "Network" tab
Go back to the login page and refresh (F5)
In the list of files, find secure.js
Click it → the code appears on the right! 
You'll see something like this:

function checkPassword(username, password) {
    if (username === "admin" && password === "strongPassword098765") {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

🎉 THERE IT IS! The admin credentials are sitting in plain text:

Field	Value
👤 Username	admin
🔑 Password	strongPassword098765

💥 Step 4: Log In!
Go back to the login page and enter:

Field	Value
Username	admin
Password	strongPassword098765

Click Login → ✅ "Log In Successful!" 

You get redirected to /admin.php and the flag is right there! 🏁 

⚡ Alternative: Skip the Login Entirely (PowerShell)
If you look closely at the JavaScript, when login succeeds it does this:

document.getElementById('adminFormHash').value = "2196812e91c29df34f5e217cfd639881";
document.getElementById('hiddenAdminForm').submit();

It sends a hash to /admin.php.  You can just send that hash directly:

Invoke-WebRequest -Uri "http://YOUR-CHALLENGE-URL:PORT/admin.php" `
  -Method POST `
  -Body "hash=2196812e91c29df34f5e217cfd639881" `
  -ContentType "application/x-www-form-urlencoded"

🎉 Flag in the response! No login needed! 

🏆 Flag

picoCTF{j5_15_7r4n5p4r3n7_b0c2c9cb}

🧠 What Did We Learn?
✅ Good Practice	❌ What Went Wrong
Check passwords on the server	Checked password in client-side JavaScript
Keep credentials secret (server-side only)	Put admin password in a .js file sent to everyone
Don't name files "secure" if they're public	secure.js is NOT secure at all! 😂

💡 The #1 rule of web security:

"The client is NOT secure."

Anything in HTML, CSS, or JavaScript is visible to the user.  You can hide it, obfuscate it, minify it — but a determined user will always find it.

Think of it this way: 🔒

Server-side = a locked safe in a bank vault ✅
Client-side = a note stuck to your monitor that anyone walking by can read ❌
🎓 Quick summary:

View Source (Ctrl + U) or Network tab (F12)
Find the secure.js file
Read the plain-text credentials
Log in → 🎉 Flag! 
